{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "206",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 70s with a history of coronary artery bypass surgery, ejection fraction of 25% and biventricular pacemaker placement was brought to the emergency department for dull chest pain of 2 hours' duration.\n\nNo detailed physical examination findings were provided in the case report.\n\n- ECG findings:    Image 1 Title: First Electrocardiogram From Patient's Current Presentation    Image Description: Shows sinus activity at 72 beats/min, normal PR interval, QRS duration of 160 milliseconds. Ventricular depolarization preceded by 2 pacing stimuli occurring less than 40 milliseconds apart. ST segment depression >1mm in V2 and V3, new discordant ST elevation of 1mm in leads II, III, and aVF, with new reciprocal ST depression <1mm in aVL.    Image 2 Title: Patient's Electrocardiogram From 3 Years Before    Image Description: Baseline ECG after biventricular pacemaker placement    Image 3 Title: Magnified and Isolated Reading From Lead V5    Image Description: Shows two ventricular pacing spikes with an offset of less than 40 milliseconds    Image 4 Title: Patient's Electrocardiogram After Catheterization    Image Description: Shows resolution of ST segment changes  - Laboratory Tests:    - Initial troponin (2 hours after symptom onset): Negative  - Cardiac Catheterization:    Revealed thrombotic occlusion of saphenous vein graft proximal to circumflex anastomosis and occlusive lesions in proximal obtuse marginal artery\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Acute ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) of the inferior wall\nB. Coronary artery disease progression with graft failure\nC. Acute inferior ST-elevation myocardial infarction (secondary to saphenous vein graft occlusion)\nD. Acute posterior and inferior myocardial infarction (STEMI equivalent) due to saphenous vein graft occlusion\n",
